  "articleBody": "When it comes to garage drywall, there are a few common pitfalls that can turn a DIY project into a headache. First off, using the wrong thickness or type of drywall is a big one. Garages often have specific fire-rating requirements, like needing Type X drywall, especially if they're attached to your house. Don't just grab any old sheet! Another frequent mistake is failing to stagger seams properly. This can lead to noticeable lines and cracks down the road. Also, poor gaps and spacing between boards can make taping and mudding a nightmare, resulting in a less-than-smooth finish. And speaking of taping and mudding, doing a weak job here, or skimping on sanding between coats, means you'll see every imperfection. It's really all about the prep and the finishing touches. These might seem like small details, but they make a huge difference in the final look and durability. If you’re not confident in your drywall skills, or if your garage has code-sensitive fire separation needs, bringing in a professional like HH Remodeling LLC can save you a lot of grief and ensure the job is done right, especially for a smooth, lasting finish.",
